WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:07.040
You have probably heard of the high&nbsp;
to late medieval guilds that existed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:10.720
all across Europe. If you have not,&nbsp;
then I made a video about that.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:16.080
However, the complicated structure of the high&nbsp;
and late medieval period occupation guilds like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:21.360
merchant guilds, or craft guilds, evolved&nbsp;
from a much more simpler style of guild that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:26.560
emerged in the early medieval period. Due&nbsp;
to this simpler structure of, oftentimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:30.240
non-occupation-centric guilds&nbsp;
of the early medieval period&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:35.120
some historians call these early guilds&nbsp;
‘gilds’ as to differentiate the two.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:41.200
A gild is an organization of multiple members&nbsp;
often, but not always, with a founding charter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:46.880
or a formal agreement and a swearing of oaths,&nbsp;
that was set up in a horizontal structure with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:51.920
certain goals in mind. These goals could&nbsp;
be anything from a simple drinking club&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:56.240
to trying to lower the crime rate in&nbsp;
a city by policing it. These gilds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:00.560
in pursuing their founding goals, could&nbsp;
act in collaboration with the state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:05.440
and or the church, or be independent&nbsp;
from them, or even act against them.

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:10.400
How these early medieval gilds formed is hard&nbsp;
to say as there are limited sources on them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:14.000
and the interpretation of those&nbsp;
sources usually depends on how a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.480
historian views the migration period and&nbsp;
the fall of the Western Roman Empire.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:24.400
To explain, there are two major historical&nbsp;
interpretations of the migration period and its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:30.880
outcome. These two interpretations are described&nbsp;
by Halsall as ‘Movers’ and ‘Shakers’. To quote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:36.320
“Movers believe that the changes of late&nbsp;
antiquity may be ascribed to the movement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:42.560
into the Roman Empire of largely Germanic&nbsp;
barbarians; the barbarians, viewed as numerically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:48.560
significant migrating ‘peoples', are at least the&nbsp;
catalyst for change. Shakers, on the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:54.080
believe that tensions and changes within the&nbsp;
Roman Empire were already shaking it to its core,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:59.280
and the barbarians, usually seen as small,&nbsp;
elite warrior groups were but a symptom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:06.080
if perhaps a focus, of these changes.“ Of course&nbsp;
not all historians fall neatly into these two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:11.360
categories and there's much more nuance to it but&nbsp;
all you need to know for this video is that there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:16.320
are these two interpretations of the migration&nbsp;
period and the fall of the Western Roman Empire.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:20.960
So with that said, historians who prefer&nbsp;
the ‘shakers’ interpretation largely see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:25.600
the early medieval gilds as stemming&nbsp;
from, if not directly connected to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:31.600
the late Roman collegia. The collegia were Roman&nbsp;
institutions closely resembling, in structure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:37.520
the well known occupation guilds of the high to&nbsp;
late middle ages. They functioned as social clubs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:43.120
religious collectives, trade unions, and even&nbsp;
sometimes as lobbying bodies. They were basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:48.960
collective institutions that could act as legal&nbsp;
entities which could hold property or sue other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:55.200
people/institutions. They often sprang up in urban&nbsp;
centres and could in reality be doing anything;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:59.840
there were collegia of grave diggers&nbsp;
and marchants, as well as of cultists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:05.040
and religious institutions. These collegia&nbsp;
also survived well into the Middle ages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:10.880
in a bit of a different form, in the Eastern Roman&nbsp;
Empire or, in other words, the Byzantine Empire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:14.640
but I won't mention them as I am&nbsp;
talking about the west in this video.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:19.600
The proponents of the ‘movers’ interpretation&nbsp;
talk about the early medieval gilds as either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:25.120
stemming from older Germanic practices&nbsp;
or being independently created within the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:31.040
newly formed early medieval Germanic kingdoms&nbsp;
after the Roman tradition of collegia died out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:35.680
Hence the early medieval gilds have&nbsp;
no connection to the Roman collegia.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:40.400
Neither of these two theories on the origins&nbsp;
of the early medieval gilds can be proven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:44.480
at the moment as not much research&nbsp;
has been done into the topic and no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:49.440
source has been discovered that would&nbsp;
outright prove or disprove one or the other.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:54.080
With that said, early medieval gilds still&nbsp;
existed so let's look at what they were and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:59.360
what they did. The early medieval gilds, unlike&nbsp;
their later counterparts, weren’t centered around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:04.560
an occupation. Meaning the gilds that were formed&nbsp;
in the early medieval Germanic kingdoms weren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:10.880
guilds of solely merchants, or cobblers, or&nbsp;
blacksmiths, etc. These gilds, rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:17.200
occupation, had largely geographical separations.&nbsp;
Meaning anyone within the geographical area of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:23.280
gilds operation could, in theory, join the gild&nbsp;
if they wanted to, no matter what their occupation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:29.120
was; and I do mean anyone. Early medieval gilds,&nbsp;
even though largely consisting of a free urban&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:35.600
class, also included surfs, clergymen, lower level&nbsp;
aristocrats, and even women which is unusual given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:40.800
the nature of later medieval guilds which often&nbsp;
outright banned women in any participation.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:46.560
Firstly, I’ll mention what I call protection&nbsp;
or military gilds but are also called ‘peace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:51.600
gilds’. The goal of some early medieval gilds&nbsp;
involved some kind of mutual protection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:55.520
sometimes with a fairly militaristic&nbsp;
character. The most famous of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.520 --> 00:05:00.720
protection gilds were London's Frith Gilds of&nbsp;
the 10th century. They were established by “a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:06.080
group of like minded individuals who kept law&nbsp;
and order within the community instead of relying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:11.040
solely on the support of the local sheriff or&nbsp;
alderman.” London wasn’t the only place where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:16.080
these kinds of gilds were established nor were&nbsp;
these kinds of gilds unique to urban centres,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:20.960
similar gilds were established across the&nbsp;
Frankish lands in rural areas. These gilds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:26.000
were a form of people’s organized law and order&nbsp;
enforcement, often in places where the state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:29.920
in other words the king, was unable to&nbsp;
provide sufficient enough protection.

