00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:39.280
Going back to protection gilds,&nbsp;
some kings embraced these gilds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:44.240
like, for example, the London ones which got&nbsp;
officially recognized by King Aethelstan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:48.240
but other protection gilds weren’t&nbsp;
as lucky and got cracked down upon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:53.600
sometimes very harshly. This was because&nbsp;
they were often viewed by kings as a form of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:59.760
unsanctioned vigilantism and or threats to royal&nbsp;
authority within the area of their operation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:04.800
For example, when the peasants of the Loire&nbsp;
valley established a protection gild in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:10.400
9th century against Viking raids from which&nbsp;
king Odo of France was unable to protect them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:15.040
they were quote ‘massacred’ by the king for&nbsp;
daring to establish an institution that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:20.800
unsanctioned by him and, according to him,&nbsp;
competed with his authority in the area.

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:26.400
Lastly the most militaristic example of these&nbsp;
protection gilds were the Saxon ‘companion’ gilds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:30.880
This kind of gild emerged in the Duchy&nbsp;
of Saxony after it was incorporated into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:37.280
the Frankish Empire in 804. It was a sort of&nbsp;
military institution of free-man and peasants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:42.640
that preceded the ‘men at arms’ of the high&nbsp;
middle ages. They acted as a protection force,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:48.000
as a raiding force, as a policing force, and&nbsp;
also as a military political force. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:54.800
the Saxon ‘companion’ gilds helped emperor Lothar&nbsp;
1st in a war against his brother Luis the German.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.800 --> 00:08:00.560
To some these military gilds may seem more&nbsp;
like roaming bands of soldiers than actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:06.160
gilds but despite their militaristic and often&nbsp;
bandit nature, the ‘companion’ gilds were still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:12.240
institutions that resembled gilds in almost every&nbsp;
way. They held meetings, collected fees, elected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:18.720
officials, and had set goals in mind, plus their&nbsp;
militaristic nature was by far not unique as even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:26.240
later medieval guilds like the Hansa could and&nbsp;
did go to war, sometimes against entire kingdoms.

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:31.360
Next I’ll talk about the most common type of&nbsp;
gilds in the early middle ages, religious gilds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:36.800
These gilds were divided between ones formed by&nbsp;
clergymen and ones by common people, of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:42.240
an overlap between the two existed. The religious&nbsp;
gilds created by clergymen were formed as sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:47.440
discussion clubs where clergymen could meet for&nbsp;
meals in either gild halls or other predetermined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:53.360
meeting places, discuss their ministry, theology,&nbsp;
and get help preparing sermons. On the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:58.720
the religious gilds formed by common people were&nbsp;
sort of prayer groups largely connected with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:04.800
parish church, and often established by wealthy&nbsp;
individuals like Barons or Bishops. The goal of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:10.240
these ‘lay’ religious gilds for the participants&nbsp;
was to become more connected to the church and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:16.160
become more attuned with god but their duty was&nbsp;
also to pray for the lives of their founder/patron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:23.120
and his family. This was not unusual in the middle&nbsp;
ages. Many wealthy people established or funded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:29.200
hospitals, abbies, monasteries, collegies, and&nbsp;
religious gilds in order that they have people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:35.200
praying for them and their family. It was a sort&nbsp;
of religious investment or insurance to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:40.240
sure that even if you did something sinful&nbsp;
you balanced it out with your investments.

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:44.640
On the other side of the spectrum from&nbsp;
the religious gilds you had social gilds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:50.000
or drinking gilds. These pretty much acted as&nbsp;
what we would today call fraternities or eating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:55.440
clubs. Members would contribute to a common fund,&nbsp;
officers would be chosen to govern the proceedings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.440 --> 00:10:01.280
and manage assets, wealthier gilds would use their&nbsp;
assets to buy/build gild halls for their events,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:07.600
and some swearing of oaths, and or rituals, and or&nbsp;
hazing of new members could have been conducted.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:13.600
These kinds of early social gilds also often&nbsp;
acted as insurance cooperatives where members&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:18.080
of the gilds swore to pay for each other's&nbsp;
legal proceedings when they got in trouble,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:23.040
or compensate each other in case of a house&nbsp;
fire, or pay for each other's funerals when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:30.240
a gild member died, etc. Social/drinking gilds&nbsp;
weren’t liked by the church much because as one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:36.160
clergyman the Alcuin of York wrote complaining to&nbsp;
the Archbishop of canterbury in the 8th century&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:40.000
“[these] curious meetings, which&nbsp;
they are accustomed to have and call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:45.520
gilds … were seducing people away from&nbsp;
prayer in church, and leading them instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:50.960
to drunken parties in surrounding hills,&nbsp;
thereby undermining epsicopal authority”.

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:58.560
Naturally the descriptions of the different types&nbsp;
of gilds I gave in this video are simplifications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:04.080
of a much more complicated reality. All&nbsp;
early medieval gilds were unique with often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:10.320
ever changing goals and members and therefore my&nbsp;
general overview of these gilds will not always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:17.120
100% apply to any specific individual gild. On top&nbsp;
of that, the main three gild types I talked about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:22.560
could and often did have large overlaps;&nbsp;
protection gilds had a fairly large social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:27.920
character to them, while social/drinking gilds&nbsp;
often had a very religious aspect to them with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:33.360
members being required to conduct frequent&nbsp;
prayers, and some religious gilds could have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:40.000
fairly wild social events. According to one source&nbsp;
“Priests [in gilds] had been attending raucous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:45.200
parties, either alone or with laymen, where&nbsp;
they drank to the saints or to their own souls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:51.200
got into fights and told stories to rowdy&nbsp;
applause; others indulged in entertainments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:57.120
involving bears and dancing girls, or even&nbsp;
demonic masks.“ Take this quote with a pinch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.120 --> 00:12:02.080
of salt however. This source comes from&nbsp;
an archbishop who was arguing against the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:07.600
existence of religious gilds and therefore his&nbsp;
account is an over-exaggeration, but it does go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:13.520
to show that the events of religious gilds could&nbsp;
have possibly gotten quite debaucherous sometimes.

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:20.160
From these early medieval gilds of Western Europe&nbsp;
evolved the later, and much more well known,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:26.720
occupation guilds. For example, the job of the&nbsp;
London peace gilds eventually got taken over by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:32.480
London Livery companies, which were essentially&nbsp;
occupation guilds. The specific social gilds that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.480 --> 00:12:37.600
acted more like insurance cooperatives&nbsp;
evolved into merchant or craft guilds which,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:43.360
on top of other things, still insured members&nbsp;
assets and still kept a very strong social dynamic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.360 --> 00:12:49.360
reminiscent of the early social gilds, just watch&nbsp;
my Life of a Hansa Merchant video to see just how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:55.280
strong these social aspects of the later medieval&nbsp;
guilds were. With that said, just like with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.280 --> 00:13:01.440
early medieval gilds and collegia it is actually&nbsp;
still debated whether the later occupation guilds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:07.200
did develop from these earlier simpler gilds.&nbsp;
However, considering that some of these early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:12.640
medieval gilds existed until the 11th and 12th&nbsp;
century, which is the same time when the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:18.400
occupation guilds started to form, I think it is&nbsp;
safe to say that even if not directly connected,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:23.840
the early medieval gilds created a social&nbsp;
institution which formed the bases upon which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.840 --> 00:13:29.760
the later more economically focused and powerful&nbsp;
occupation guilds, like the Hansa, were created.

